From: Stained Glass OS <ke7oxh@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] qedit: implement the editing timeline object model

The timeline object had no state: its group list, insert mode, enable flags,
frame rate, default transition/effect, interest range, dirty tracking and the
per-node properties all returned E_NOTIMPL. Now:

IAMTimeline keeps an ordered list of group nodes (AddGroup takes a reference
and refuses non-group nodes, nodes that are already in a timeline and foreign
objects with E_INVALIDARG; RemGroupFromList, ClearAllGroups, GetGroup with
index checks, GetGroupCount), the insert mode (insert by default, overlay
accepted), transition and effect enables (on by default), default frame rate
(15), interest range, default transition/effect as GUID or string,
duration (the latest group end), dirty flag and range collected from the
groups, GetCountOfType and ValidateSourceNames (there are no source nodes).

IAMTimelineObj stores start/stop (negative or reversed times are
E_INVALIDARG, REFERENCE_TIME and seconds variants, FixTimes snaps to the frame
grid of the group or timeline), dirty range (changes mark the old and new
extent dirty), muted/locked flags, user id and a per-node generation id, user
name, user data, property setter, sub-object (an object or a CLSID that is
instantiated on first use), Remove/RemoveAll, GetGroupIBelongTo and
GetEmbedDepth. IAMTimelineGroup is only exposed on group nodes and keeps
output frame rate, name, preview mode, buffering, media type (copied with its
format block), the smart-recompression format with its dirty flag, and the
group's priority/timeline back references. A group outlives its timeline.
SetRecompFormatFromSource still reports E_NOTIMPL: it needs source nodes.
